  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Prem Singh Rathore vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh on 23 February, 2026 Author: Gurpal Singh Ahluwalia Bench: G. S. Ahluwalia 1 In The High Court Of Madhya Pradesh At Gwalior Before Hon'Ble Shri Justice G. S. Ahluwalia On The 23rd Of February, 2026 Miscellaneous Second Appeal No. 2 of 2026 Prem Singh Rathore Versus The State Of Madhya Pradesh Appearance: Shri Harshad Bahrani - Advocate for appellant. Shri C.P. Singh - Govt. Advocate for respondent/State. Judgment",
    "facts": "This miscellaneous second appeal under Section 71(6) of Food Safety and Standards Act (in short \"FSSA\") has been filed against the judgment dated 21/11/2025 passed by Principal District Judge, Gwalior/Appellate Tribunal in RCA No. 116/2025, as well as order dated 27/12/2024 passed by Additional District Magistrate/Adjudicating Officer, District Gwalior in Case No. Q/Food Safety/113/2024. 2. According to prosecution case, on 27/9/2023 at about 3:00 pm, the Food Safety Officer, along with his team, carried out the inspection of Jaigurudev Milk Dairy, and during inspection, it was found that 12 kg of paneer, 15 kg of curd, 15 kg of ghee in a container were kept for human consumption and sale. Since the Food Safety Officer had a suspicion that paneer and dahi might be substandard, therefore, samples were collected. The samples were sent to the Food Analyst. The samples were found to be substandard. Notice was issued to appellant but he did not exercise his power 2 MSA-2-2026 under Section 46(4) of FSSA and Rule 2.4.2(6) of the Food Safety and Standards Rules, 2011, and accordingly, a complaint was filed. A show- cause notice was issued by the Adjudicating Officer. Appellant denied that any search was carried out. It was claimed that the samples were not collected in accordance with law, therefore, they were found to be of substandard quality. 3. The Adjudicating Officer, after recording the evidence of parties, came to a conclusion that the paneer and curd were found to be substandard, and accordingly, appellant has violated the provisions of Section 51 of the FSSA and imposed a fine of Rs.2,00,000/-. 5. Challenging the orders passed by the Adjudicating Officer as well as Appellate Tribunal below, it is submitted by counsel for appellant that appellant is a very small business operator. Although in the grounds raised in the memo of appeal it was alleged that there was a delay in conducting the test, but no arguments to that effect were advanced by counsel for appellant.",
